-- title:
--   Adic completion at a maximal ideal is complete Noetherian local
-- statement:
--   Let $C$ be a commutative Noetherian ring and let $\mathfrak n \subseteq C$ be a maximal ideal; write $\widehat C =$ `AdicCompletion 𝔫 C` for the $\mathfrak n$-adic completion of $C$ itself (no localisation is taken), regarded as a $C$-algebra via `algebraMap`. The assertion is the conjunction of: (i) $\widehat C$ is a Noetherian ring; and (ii) there exists a local-ring structure on $\widehat C$ — the statement is packaged as an existential over the class `IsLocalRing`, so that a consumer may install the instance — for which four further facts hold: the maximal ideal of $\widehat C$ equals the ideal $\mathfrak n\widehat C$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak n$ under $C \to \widehat C$; $\widehat C$ is adically complete for this maximal ideal (the canonical map to the limit of the quotients by its powers is bijective, in the `IsAdicComplete` sense); the contraction of the maximal ideal along $C \to \widehat C$ is exactly $\mathfrak n$; and the composite of $C \to \widehat C$ with the residue map $\widehat C \to \widehat C/\mathfrak m_{\widehat C}$ is surjective, so that the residue field of $\widehat C$ is identified with $C/\mathfrak n$.
--
--   This is the standard description of the completion of a Noetherian ring at a maximal ideal as a complete Noetherian local ring with the same residue field. It is used downstream whenever a deformation-theoretic or étale-algebra argument requires a complete local base together with an identification of its residue field with $C/\mathfrak n$, for instance to transport separability of residue extensions, as in the Artinian-quotient and adjoined-root constructions that cite it.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AdicCompletion_isNoetherianRing_and_exists_isLocalRing_maximalIdeal_eq_map_of_isMaximal.lean

